{"name":"Niagara","hex":"#29a98b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Niagara","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river (the Niagara River) which flows from Lake Erie, over Niagara Falls into Lake Ontario; the geographical region of the United States and Canada in proximity to this river.","A ghost town in British Columbia, Canada."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flood, torrent, or outpouring, especially one of massive proportions.","A Niagara grape."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Niagara"}],"entries":[{"title":"Niagara Falls","blocks":[{"text":"Niagara Falls is a group of three waterfalls at the southern end of Niagara Gorge, spanning the border between the Canadian province of Ontario and the U.S. state of New York. The largest of the three is Horseshoe Falls, which straddles the international border of the two countries. It is also known as the Canadian Falls. The smaller American Falls and Bridal Veil Falls lie within the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/3Falls_Niagara.jpg/330px-3Falls_Niagara.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niagara_Falls","wikidata":"Q34221"},{"title":"Niagara River","blocks":[{"text":"The Niagara River flows north from Lake Erie to Lake Ontario, forming part of the border between Ontario, Canada, to the west, and New York, United States, to the east. The river, occasionally described as a strait, is approximately 58 kilometres long and is known for the Niagara Falls. Over the past 12,000 years, the falls have moved roughly 11 kilometres (6.8 mi) upstream from the leading edge of the Niagara Escarpment, creating a gorge below the falls. Today, the diversion of the river for electricity generation has significantly slowed the rate of erosion. The total elevation drop along the river is 99 metres (325 ft). The Niagara downstream from the falls, includes the Niagara Whirlpool and additional rapids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Whirlpool_Bridge_%282936545446%29.jpg/330px-Whirlpool_Bridge_%282936545446%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niagara_River","wikidata":"Q182343"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/1280px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Canadian Horseshoe Falls at right","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/American_Falls_Niagara_Falls_USA_from_Skylon_Tower_on_2002-05-28.png/500px-American_Falls_Niagara_Falls_USA_from_Skylon_Tower_on_2002-05-28.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/American_Falls_Niagara_Falls_USA_from_Skylon_Tower_on_2002-05-28.png/1280px-American_Falls_Niagara_Falls_USA_from_Skylon_Tower_on_2002-05-28.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"h